 offered  to rewrite all of connect from scratch  and the first time he filed that issue  was like that's interesting why would  you want to do that what we have works  just fine but we kept talking and  iterating through this process and he  eventually proved that his version was a  lot better in a lot of ways so we  iterated on that through the rest of the  year and by the end of 2018 we published  this implementation as version 5 and  actually did have a lot of performance  improvements and it fixed a lot of bugs  in the process so some interesting  points about version 5 version 4 had a  lot of recurring bugs that would only  happen in certain edge cases where it  was possible for a child component to  subscribe to the store before the parent  and if something happened like data for  a list item was deleted the child might  try to read that data from the store  even though it was gone and that could  result in an error being thrown from the  map state function  I refer to this as the zombie child bug  problem and so in version 5 we fixed  that by using this internal subscription  class to cascade the notifications  step-by-step down through the tree and  so this fixed the problems because we  ensure that a parent component always  subscribes to this store before the  children do and so if an item gets  deleted the parent will stop rendering  that child before the child can run its  map state function so this was a really  big help  version 5 also added something called  connect advanced this was really the  guts of connect but less opinionated  about how to turn store state and  wrapper props into the actual props for  the child component now it's interesting  because on the one hand we reimplemented  Connect has a certain set of options to  connect advanced on the other hand  almost nobody uses this and in fact  there's some recent discussion we're  probably going to deprecated and  eventually remove that function because  no one even knows it exists another  interesting point about version 5 is  that all the logic for determining if  the component should rerender has been  moved outside of the component itself  and into a set of custom memorized  selector functions and so all the logic  of running your map state and map  dispatch and calculating the new child  props actually happens outside of the  react component tree and we only  actually get react involved once we know  that the child component needs to  rerender and this is ultimately the  reason why version 5 was a lot faster  than version 4 so we will skip past the  code snippets again and move on to  version 6  so version 5 came out at the end of 2016  and lasted for a couple years but by  early 2018 we started having some  discussions with the react team  about this new concurrent mode thing  that they were talking about and flarn  II will be talking about that surely and  give it a lot more detail on that topic  and so one of the big concerns that the  react team expressed was this idea of  tearing what if during one render  sequence part of the component tree  reads data a from the store an action is  dispatched in the middle and now the  rest of the tree reads a different set  of data from the store you would have  two different parts of the tree  rendering with different data and Andrew  Clarke especially expressed a lot of  concerns about this problem version 5  also throughs a number of warnings in  react strict mode and when we tried to  eliminate those in a modified version 5  the performance was abysmal we couldn't  fix it and finally reacted off had  released a new conversion of their  context API in 16.3 and we wanted to  switch over to that so our goals for  version 6 we wanted to switch from old  context to new context we wanted to fix  the strict mode warnings Wow we wanted  to drop the the very clunky ref  forwarding implementation that we had  and we had the general goal of maybe we  can be more compatible with concurrent  mode somehow so I collaborated with the  user named Greg beaver on the version 6  implementation and we both kind of came  up with our own separate approaches for  how this might work we did some  comparisons and eventually settled on  Greg's implementation as the final  version and so we put out version 6 at  the end of 2018 two years after the  release of version 5 so one of the  biggest changes in version 6 is that  rather than having every component be a  separate subscriber to the store we only  had one subscriber and that was actually  in the provider component itself and it  actually passed the entire store state  down via reacts new context API and part  of the reason for this was  that by having the entire component tree  always seen the same copy of the store  state at the same time it would  eliminate that tearing problem in  addition when because we react always  rear Enders top-down we actually were  able to drop that custom subscription  class code because that's just how react  updates anyway and the new context API  always propagates updates even if you  return even if you have components that  skip updating so I thought this is great  it's all built-in to react it works for  free we can simplify our own codebase we  still use the same selector functions as  version 5 but now we actually had to  call them directly in the render method  of the wrapper component again because  you can only access data from context  directly in there and so we went up  using that same memo Eyes child element  trick from version 4 to try to optimize  how the components re render we also  dropped our width ref option and used  reacts new forward ref API so that you  could just put a ref on the wrapper  component and actually get back your own  component instance as a result so that  brings us up to version 7 when version 6  was in development we had some concerns  about performance and so I put together  a benchmarks repository that would try  to compare version 5 and version 6 and  try to make sure that things didn't  really get slower but during the  benchmarks and the development we saw  that version 6 was actually slower in  several of the scenarios now these were  very very artificial stress test  scenarios it's like how many components  can I render on screen before it starts  to slow down in some way but the goal  was to have some kind of objective  numbers that we could compare so what we  saw was that version 6 was slower in  these stress tests but our hope was that  in real-world apps we could put it out  and there wouldn't actually be a  difference but after version six is  release several people did file issues  and said that in their actual real  applications they did see slowdowns of  some kind so we started investigating  those performance issues and it really  comes down to how version 5 in version 6  handle updates in version 5 every  component subscribes the checks for  updates are done outside of react and  react only gets involved once we know  the component needs to re-render in  version 6 we always have to get react  involved because the provider is calling  set state every time there's a new state  value and even if and so that means  there's a rerender working top-down even  if no components actually care about the  change to the state and so even if react  isn't doing a lot of work in that render  that's still a lot more work than just  running a couple of selector functions  there are also some concerns about the  fact that you there was no more store as  a prop in version 6  since the connected components didn't  subscribe themselves there there was no  point in having store as a prop and so  we'd removed that and some users  expressed concerns that they couldn't  test their connected components  effectively and finally this was a  blocker for our ability to maybe create  some kind of a redux hooks API because  context always causes that component to  update there's no way to avoid  rear-ending when the context value  changes and there was a mile long thread  in the react repo where we were  discussing with some of the react team  members what an API for avoiding updates  from context might look like and  eventually a sebastian mark Baga said my  personal summary is that new context is  ready to be used for low frequency  updates it's not ready to be used as a  replacement for flux like state  propagation which was a problem because  we  using it for flux like state propagation  so that was a problem so in early  February this year I published a road  map issue saying okay we're gonna come  up with a version seven and we're gonna  fix these problems I don't know how  we're going to fix them yet but we're  gonna fix them somehow and so the  primary goals were run as least as fast  as version five bring back and fix the  problems with stores prop hopefully work  okay with concurrent react but maybe not  because it's not out yet hopefully avoid  the zombie child issues and hopefully  act as a foundation for eventually  building a hooks API so I wound up  building the alpha on this entirely by  myself I just had a couple weeks free I  was able to hack on something and put it  together and again after a lot of  discussion and iteration and alphas we  eventually put this out as a final  release in April and that is what's out  there right now so the key points for  this one we brought back direct  subscriptions so every connected  component is now subscribing to the  store directly again that did mean that  we had to bring back the custom  subscription class that we had from  version 5 I literally copy and paste it  from the version 5 code tree and so the  result of this is that version 7 is at  least as fast as version 5 in all of our  benchmarks if not faster the other  really interesting thing is that Connect  uses hooks internally previously Connect  was a class component and now it is a  function component that uses hooks the  other really interesting thing here is  that we now use reacts batched updates  API internally many of you have heard  have seen that if I call set state more  than once in a click handler react will  group those updates together into a  single  and that's because react wraps your  event callback with this batched updates  API and so we now actually use that  inside of connect so that the updates  caused by the redux dispatch are  actually batched together and cause  fewer internal rear Enders we also now  re export that function as part of our  public API so that if you're dispatching  multiple redux actions outside of react  like in a thunk or something  you can now wrap those in this batch  call and it should only cause one  rerender instead of multiple rear Enders  so let's actually look at a couple of  these code snippets real fast so you can  see that connect itself is now a  function component that uses hooks  internally for its implementation you  can see some fun logic for actually  running that selector and trying to  calculate what the new child props  should actually look like and we  actually have to force a rerender when  an action is dispatched and we've  calculated that the child props have  changed and finally we do actually use  that same memorized child element trick  except that this time we can now use  reacts built-in use memo hook to do that  work rather than trying to memorize that  logic in some separate way so that  brings us up to version 7.1 when hooks  first came out the react hooks FAQ said  that other libraries might want to  provide their own hooks like maybe some  kind of a use Redux hook or something  like that  and so like 30 seconds after the  announcement issues were being filed on  the react Redux repo saying when are you  going to give us hooks but we had to get  this version 7 done and out the door  before  we could even really consider what a  Redux hooks API might look like so there  was very very strong demand from the  community in fact at one point I put up  a poll on Twitter saying so now that  version sevens done what would people  like me to focus my time on should I  work on a reactor Redux API should I  work on improving the Redux Docs should  I work on Redux starter kit and like 85%  of people said hooks hooks it's like  okay okay I can take a hint so there  were several questions that we had to  solve what would the actual API even  look like how do you handle selectors  that need to depend on props for the  component how can we avoid this zombie  child bug again and like what if some  solution that we come up with maybe  requires es6 use like proxies so we had  several very very long discussion  threads like over 200 comments apiece  and after comparing a lot of existing  third-party implementations and some  suggestions from the community we  eventually settled on an implementation  from a user named Jonathan Ziller and we  put out our first alpha back in April  and the initial alpha had five different  hooks use selector use actions used  Redux use dispatch and use store and we  continue to iterate on the API as we  went through a couple more alphas we  dropped the use Redux hook which  basically was the equivalent of used  selector plus use actions like Connect  because it really didn't provide any  real benefit we also dropped use actions  because Dan felt that it was too much  overhead and not really helpful when  used in a hooks kind of a world and we  also changed how you selector calculates  its equality to determine if a rerender  is actually necessary  so after multiple iterations we put out  a release candidate in wait May and  we've been waiting to hear a bit of  feedback on that  and so what the what do these hooks  actually look like in action so you  would still you still need to put a  provider around your entire app same as  always but as you can see over on the  right we now have a used selector  function where you can extract data from  the store  there is a used dispatch hook that gives  you the stored dispatch reference  directly and so then I could you know  say create a quick hint a click handler  where we call dispatch and use some kind  of an action creator so hopefully it's  fairly fairly simple and lightweight and  should work pretty well in your  applications and that's why I am pleased  to announce that as of late last night  react Redux version 7.1 with hooks is  now final so III will say that as a  maintainer I have discovered that no  matter how much you ask for feedback  during + alpha or beta the best way to  get feedback on anything is to publish a  final version at which point everybody  starts filing issues that something is  broken so given that the code here is  relatively simple I'm hoping there won't  be many issues but please try it out in  your own applications like today and  told me everything works just fine ok so  what does the future hold we're still  really not sure exactly how this react  concurrent mode thing is going to work  out hopefully for any can answer some of  those questions for us it's very  possible that react Redux and concurrent  mode might not play all that wall  together we don't know we're gonna have  to wait and see as far as actual changes  to the library we're kind of hoping that  we won't need any more changes for a  while but if there is keep an eye on the  issues I'm sure there will be  discussions alright I am out of time and  I actually managed to finish things  without having hurry too much so my  slides have links to some additional  discussion and information from the docs  I'll have those up on my blog hopefully  sometime later today thank you very much  for your time and enjoy the conference  [Applause]
 

